# Local defaults, loaded by `npm run dev`. Copy to .env to override — .env is gitignored and .env
# is loaded last, so it wins. Neither file overrides a variable already set in the shell.
#
# Real secrets never live in the repo. In staging and prod every value here comes from Secret
# Manager through infra/set-secret.sh (SPEC.md, "Security defaults"; ADR-0003).
DATABASE_URL=postgres://dona:dona@127.0.0.1:5434/dona
PORT=3000

# The golden set embeds and reads through the real provider (`npm run evals`, `npm run measure`).
# Absent, the retrieval and grounding cases skip and say so, and the extraction golden set does not
# run; CI sets REQUIRE_EMBEDDINGS=1 so there they fail instead.
#
# **Never a value in this file.** Put it in `.env`, which is gitignored, loaded last and wins — both
# scripts load it (`--env-file-if-exists=.env`), so the key reaches the run without being typed into
# a terminal, pasted into a prompt or left in a shell's history. Exporting it in your shell still
# works and still wins over nothing; a shell export and `.env` are alternatives, not a sequence.
#
# Those two scripts deliberately do NOT load this file, unlike `dev` and `migrate` above. They reach
# a database through `pg-support.ts`'s own default, and loading DATABASE_URL from here would change
# which database the gates run against as a side effect of a change about an API key.
# OPENAI_API_KEY=

# The docs bucket (slice 3.2). Deliberately unset here: locally there is no bucket, so
# createConfiguredStore falls back to the in-memory store and src/serve.ts prints `docs: memory` on
# the boot line. In staging and prod the deploy injects it (DOCS_BUCKET=dona-v5-<env>-docs), and a
# deployed revision whose boot line says `memory` is as wrong as a `-dev` version string.
# DOCS_BUCKET=

# The Document AI OCR processor (slice 4.1). Unset here on the same argument: locally there is no
# processor, createConfiguredOcr returns unconfigured, and src/serve.ts prints `ocr: unconfigured`.
# Staging and prod inject DOCUMENT_AI_PROCESSOR (looked up by display name dona-ocr-<env> in eu).
# The version is a config_settings row, not this file.
# DOCUMENT_AI_PROCESSOR=
# DOCUMENT_AI_LOCATION=eu
# GOOGLE_CLOUD_PROJECT=dona-v5

# Google sign-in (slice 5.1b). Deliberately unset here: with no OAuth client
# createConfiguredIdentity returns unconfigured, src/serve.ts prints `identity: unconfigured` on the
# boot line, and /staff/login says so rather than starting a redirect to nowhere. To sign in
# locally, put the two values in .env (gitignored) — the client is created by hand in the Cloud
# console, and its authorised redirect URI must be exactly
# http://127.0.0.1:3000/staff/auth/callback. Staging and prod inject both from Secret Manager
# (<env>-google-oauth-client-id / -secret, through infra/set-secret.sh). Never a value in this file.
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
# GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The Workspace domain to require, when Dona Dom's answer lands (open question 12). Unset means the
# allowlist in staff_account is the only fence, which is the honest default until it does.
# STAFF_GOOGLE_HD=
# Where Google is told to send an operator back. Absent, the request's own origin is used, which is
# what `npm run dev` wants. The deploy sets it to the revision's own URL.
# STAFF_BASE_URL=
# The tenant host, hostname only. Absent, the process uses tenant.localhost, which is this machine
# and never the office. Staging and prod set the real name when the portal has one.
# TENANT_HOST=
# The portal writes its sign-in code to the server log on a laptop, where no provider is
# configured (#273). Do not set this on staging or production: the process refuses to boot.
# PORTAL_CODE_LOG=

# Outlook, the staging test mailbox (#188). All four must be set or the process boots
# `outlook: unconfigured` and does not poll. They are Secret Manager values
# (<env>-graph-tenant-id, -graph-client-id, -graph-client-secret, -graph-mailbox) through
# infra/set-secret.sh. Never a value in this file. Production is a later ticket and is not mounted.
# GRAPH_TENANT_ID=
# GRAPH_CLIENT_ID=
# GRAPH_CLIENT_SECRET=
# GRAPH_MAILBOX=
# The secret's expiry, YYYY-MM-DD (#198). Optional: without it the calls list warns that the expiry
# is unknown; from 30 days before it, the list warns to renew. <env>-graph-secret-expires.
# GRAPH_SECRET_EXPIRES=

# WhatsApp, Meta's developer test number (#193). All four must be set or the
# process boots `whatsapp: unconfigured` and does not accept webhooks. They are
# Secret Manager values (<env>-whatsapp-app-secret, -whatsapp-verify-token,
# -whatsapp-access-token, -whatsapp-phone-number-id) through
# infra/set-secret.sh. Never a value in this file. Production is a later ticket
# and is not mounted.
# WHATSAPP_APP_SECRET=
# WHATSAPP_VERIFY_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_ACCESS_TOKEN=
# WHATSAPP_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
